WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:01.730
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Monitoring, analyzing,

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.550
and optimizing costs have become
an important part of managing

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.660
your cloud environment.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.610
But it can be challenging
to work with all

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.410
your cost and usage data.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.250
On this episode of
"Beyond Your Bill,"

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.550
we'll look at exporting your
billing data to BigQuery

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.620
and how to create
custom dashboards.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.830
Our last video covered how
to explore your GCP costs

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.080
through the billing reports
that you can easily access

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.610
through the Cloud Console.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.159
Now let's look at
exporting it to BigQuery,

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.740
and then create a dashboard
using Data Studio to further

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.777
explore it.

00:00:35.777 --> 00:00:37.860
It's important to note
that these actions can only

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.860
be performed by a
billing administrator.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.467
As we mentioned back in the
resource organization video,

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:46.050
you'll want to enable BigQuery
export for your billing account

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.440
as soon as possible.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.763
Ideally, you do this as
soon as you create it.

00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:51.930
That's because the export
will start collecting data

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.760
as soon as you
enable it, and you

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.030
can't get detailed billing data
from before it's been enabled.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.967
With that being said, let me
show you how to enable it.

00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:02.550
In the Cloud Console, click on
Billing, and choose the billing

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.930
account you want to work with.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.930
You may have to click on
Manage Billing Accounts

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.438
first, then click on
Billing Export on the left.

00:01:08.438 --> 00:01:10.980
Here's where you can see if the
billing exports for BigQuery,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.110
or file exports, are enabled.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.350
While there is an option to do
a CSV or JSON export under file

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.870
export, we don't actually
recommend doing this,

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.880
because it doesn't contain
as much information

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.380
as the BigQuery export.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.660
The more information you
have, the more analysis

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.100
you'll be able to do.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.160
So go ahead and click on Edit
Settings to choose a project,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.787
and then choose a
BigQuery dataset.

00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:32.370
If you don't have
any projects, you'll

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.290
need to create one
with BigQuery enabled,

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.360
then create a BigQuery dataset.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.700
Creating a dataset is as easy
as going into the BigQuery

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.620
interface, clicking
on your project,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.020
and clicking on the
Create Dataset button.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.760
If you already have
a bunch of projects,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.580
feel free to make a new one
just for this export or all

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.515
of the other cost management
efforts that you're working on.

00:01:50.515 --> 00:01:52.140
Once you know which
project and dataset

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.360
you want to use, choose
them in the export settings

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.193
and hit Save.

00:01:55.193 --> 00:01:56.610
You should see a
screen that looks

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.568
like this, which means
that you've successfully

00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:00.580
enabled the export process.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.910
You can click the dataset
name to jump into BigQuery,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.950
but you probably won't
see anything yet.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.200
It may take a few hours
to start seeing data,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.440
since different services have
different update frequencies.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.230
Also worth noting is that you
may incur some nominal BigQuery

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.100
costs for storing this data.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.770
You can quickly
calculate any costs

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.660
by looking at the
BigQuery pricing page

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.410
and seeing how much
data you're generating

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.840
every day with the export.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.330
You can also set a budget
specific to BigQuery

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.680
if you want to keep closer
track of your spending.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.430
We'll cover this in more
detail in a later video.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.070
Once the data starts showing up,
you can run queries against it

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.958
through the BigQuery UI.

00:02:33.958 --> 00:02:36.000
Here's a sample query that
shows the amount spent

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.380
on each service by month since
BigQuery export was enabled.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.130
This is a simple place
to start, but there

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.890
are lots of columns you can work
with, like Location, Project,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.060
and Labels.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.770
From here, you can
build and run queries

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.750
as you see fit to
analyze your data

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.125
or even start
making predictions.

00:02:51.125 --> 00:02:52.500
Now, let's talk
about another way

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.440
to analyze your billing data,
creating a custom dashboard

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.820
with Data Studio.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.240
Data Studio is an interactive
analytics tool built into GCP.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.080
And it can integrate easily with
BigQuery to pull data and then

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.417
let you visualize it.

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.000
The quickest way to
set up a dashboard

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.750
is to start with the
sample billing report

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.390
that we've put together for
you as a starting point.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.630
You can do this by visiting
the Sample Billing Report

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.850
and clicking the
How to Copy tab.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.183
By following the steps
outlined on that page,

00:03:17.183 --> 00:03:18.600
you'll set up some
special queries

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.600
and connect them to your
own copy of the billing

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.290
report in Data Studio.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.810
This sample report is ready
to go, with charts and filters

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.580
to help you visualize your data.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.230
On the Resources page,
you can filter by label.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.110
So you'll easily be able to see
how your costs are broken down.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.350
For example, if you chose
to label your resources

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.920
by environment, you
can see how much

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.920
you're spending on
production versus development

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.632
environments.

00:03:38.632 --> 00:03:40.590
This is another reason
it's important to choose

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.380
naming conventions that are
clear and easily understood

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.700
by everyone.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.470
You're also able to
build your own reports

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.550
by using Data Studio.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.140
So I'll quickly
show you how to get

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.060
started with a basic report.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.980
First, head to the
Data Studio page

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.590
and choose a blank report
to start from scratch.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.480
Once it's been created,
look for the option

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.630
to Create New Data Source,
and then choose BigQuery.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.800
From here, you just need
to select the same project

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.090
and dataset as well as the
table that BigQuery created.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.250
After choosing it, click
Connect, and then click

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.600
Add to report.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.770
Once the data source
is added to the report,

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.937
you'll be able to reference
it with different graphs

00:04:13.937 --> 00:04:14.640
and filters.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.529
As an example, you
can insert a new pie

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.390
chart that shows how much
you've spent on each service.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.290
Click on Insert, and then Pie
Chart to add it to the report,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.330
then click on it
to customize it.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.540
By changing the Dimension to
service.description and then

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.640
the Metric to Cost,
you can quickly

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.515
see how your costs are split
between different services.

00:04:32.515 --> 00:04:34.140
Different graphs have
different fields.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.000
And you can create
interactive elements

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.410
to dynamically update them.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.260
Exporting your data to BigQuery
lets you analyze your cost data

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.780
using BigQuery and SQL,
as well as setting you up

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.750
to visualize your costs with
Data Studio or other data

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.130
visualization tools.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.890
In the next videos, we'll look
at advanced cost control tools,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.360
like budgets and quotas.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

